.TH CJSON_DELETE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cjson_delete \- remove and free the DOM node at a dot-separated path
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

ccol_retval_t cjson_delete(cjson root, const char *path);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Navigates to the parent of the node that path addresses, removes the
child and frees its whole subtree.  The path uses the same
dot-separated syntax as cjson_get and cjson_set.

For a dictionary parent, the macro uses the leaf component as the key
to remove.  For a list parent, the leaf component must be a '#N'
token; for example, "#2" removes index 2.

The macro splits the path at the LAST dot that is not escaped, which
is why "a.b.c" targets the key "c" inside the node at "a.b".  When
path holds no dot, root is the parent.

As in cjson_get and cjson_set, the escape sequences '\e.' and '\e\e'
give a literal dot or a literal backslash in a key name.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_retval_t.  Returns ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when root or path is NULL; for an empty path or an
empty path component, which a leading dot, a trailing dot or two dots
together give; when the leaf component is not valid for the type of
the parent; and for a malformed "#N" index.
ccol_key_not_found when the parent path is absent, or when the leaf
key or index has a correct form but is absent.
ccol_not_enough_memory when an internal allocation fails.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cjson_delete(doc, "users.#0.address");
cjson_delete(doc, "config.debug");
cjson_delete(doc, "items.#2");
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_get (3),
.BR cjson_set (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_remove (3),
.BR cjson_list_remove (3)
